    50   * A Mesh holds vertices composed of attributes specified by a {@link 
VertexAttributes} instance.
    51   * The vertices are held either in VRAM in form of vertex buffer objects
or in RAM in form of vertex
    52   * arrays. The former variant is more performant and is preferred over 
vertex arrays if hardware
    53   * supports it.
    54   *
    55   * <p>Meshes are automatically managed. If the OpenGL context is lost 
all vertex buffer objects get
    56   * invalidated and must be reloaded when the context is recreated. This 
only happens on Android when
    57   * a user switches to another application or receives an incoming call. 
A managed Mesh will be
    58   * reloaded automagically so you don't have to do this manually.
    59   *
    60   * <p>A Mesh consists of vertices and optionally indices which specify 
which vertices define a
    61   * triangle. Each vertex is composed of attributes such as position, 
normal, color or texture
    62   * coordinate. Note that not all of this attributes must be given, 
except for position which is
    63   * non-optional. Each attribute has an alias which is used when 
rendering a Mesh in OpenGL ES 2.0.
    64   * The alias is used to bind a specific vertex attribute to a shader 
attribute. The shader source
    65   * and the alias of the attribute must match exactly for this to work.
